WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.559
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level one.

2
00:00:05.859 --> 00:00:09.211
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.511 --> 00:00:15.890
Unit seven: Crime & Safety News. Today you will report carefully with passive forms and 'allegedly'.

4
00:00:16.190 --> 00:00:18.726
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:22.888
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:23.750 --> 00:00:25.099
Investigation.

7
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:29.546
Investigation means a careful search for facts about an event.

8
00:00:30.150 --> 00:00:32.463
The police opened an investigation.

9
00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.911
Your turn. Say: Investigation.

10
00:00:38.767 --> 00:00:40.116
Investigation.

11
00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:41.839
Suspect.

12
00:00:42.850 --> 00:00:45.964
Suspect means a person thought to have committed a crime.

13
00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:49.200
The suspect was questioned for two hours.

14
00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:52.598
Your turn. Say: Suspect.

15
00:00:55.433 --> 00:00:56.423
Suspect.

16
00:00:57.183 --> 00:00:58.045
Arrest.

17
00:00:59.050 --> 00:01:02.826
Arrest means the act of taking someone to be questioned by police.

18
00:01:03.417 --> 00:01:05.242
The arrest took place at dawn.

19
00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:08.572
Your turn. Say: Arrest.

20
00:01:11.433 --> 00:01:12.539
Arrest.

21
00:01:13.283 --> 00:01:14.110
Verdict.

22
00:01:15.117 --> 00:01:18.254
Verdict means the decision of a court at the end of a trial.

23
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:21.035
The verdict was announced on Friday.

24
00:01:21.650 --> 00:01:24.360
Your turn. Say: Verdict.

25
00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:27.946
Verdict.

26
00:01:28.683 --> 00:01:29.951
Law enforcement.

27
00:01:30.950 --> 00:01:35.155
Law enforcement means the police and others who make sure laws are obeyed.

28
00:01:35.750 --> 00:01:38.226
Law enforcement agencies worked together.

29
00:01:38.817 --> 00:01:42.050
Your turn. Say: Law enforcement.

30
00:01:44.900 --> 00:01:46.424
Law enforcement.

31
00:01:47.183 --> 00:01:48.266
Evidence.

32
00:01:49.250 --> 00:01:52.550
Evidence means facts or objects that help prove something.

33
00:01:53.150 --> 00:01:55.033
The evidence was shown to the judge.

34
00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.954
Your turn. Say: Evidence.

35
00:02:00.800 --> 00:02:01.859
Evidence.

36
00:02:02.617 --> 00:02:03.896
Public safety.

37
00:02:04.883 --> 00:02:08.404
Public safety means the protection of people from danger and crime.

38
00:02:09.017 --> 00:02:11.585
Public safety is the police's first duty.

39
00:02:12.183 --> 00:02:15.230
Your turn. Say: Public safety.

40
00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:19.149
Public safety.

41
00:02:19.883 --> 00:02:20.977
Court case.

42
00:02:21.983 --> 00:02:24.981
Court case means a legal matter decided by a judge.

43
00:02:25.583 --> 00:02:27.687
The court case lasted three weeks.

44
00:02:28.283 --> 00:02:31.063
Your turn. Say: Court case.

45
00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:35.110
Court case.

46
00:02:35.900 --> 00:02:39.771
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:40.617 --> 00:02:46.915
Visal, the report says a man 'was arrested' and 'is accused of theft'. Why not just 'he stole'?

48
00:02:48.017 --> 00:02:57.380
Until a court gives a verdict, he is only a suspect. Reporters use the passive and words like 'accused' and 'allegedly' to stay fair.

49
00:02:58.483 --> 00:03:00.344
So the police are not named?

50
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:11.370
Often they are: 'Law enforcement officers were called to the scene.' The passive keeps the focus on the person arrested and the facts.

51
00:03:12.483 --> 00:03:14.309
Is this a rule of journalism?

52
00:03:15.417 --> 00:03:21.726
Yes. Ethical reporters wait for evidence and a verdict before they call anyone guilty.

53
00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:24.518
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:25.383 --> 00:03:28.660
Grammar focus: Passive and careful reporting words.

55
00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:30.135
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:33.860
A man was arrested after a police investigation.

57
00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:37.667
The suspect is accused of stealing three motorbikes.

58
00:03:38.583 --> 00:03:40.687
The evidence was presented to the court.

59
00:03:41.633 --> 00:03:48.581
Before a verdict, write 'is accused of', 'is alleged to have' or 'allegedly'. Never call a suspect guilty in a news report.

60
00:03:49.317 --> 00:03:53.084
Be careful! Don't say: The suspect is guilty of theft.

61
00:03:55.033 --> 00:03:57.997
Say: The suspect is accused of theft.

62
00:03:58.297 --> 00:04:00.412
Before a verdict, write 'accused'.

63
00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:03.934
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:04.733 --> 00:04:06.048
Fair Crime Reporting.

65
00:04:06.348 --> 00:04:10.124
Crime news must inform the public without hurting an innocent person.

66
00:04:11.133 --> 00:04:15.861
After an investigation, a suspect was arrested and is accused of theft.

67
00:04:16.867 --> 00:04:22.326
Law enforcement officers collected evidence, which will be presented in a court case next month.

68
00:04:23.333 --> 00:04:28.166
Until the verdict, reporters say 'allegedly' because the suspect is still presumed innocent.

69
00:04:29.167 --> 00:04:32.687
Careful wording protects both public safety and fairness.

70
00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:37.651
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:04:38.517 --> 00:04:41.573
A man blank arrested after a police investigation.

72
00:04:41.873 --> 00:04:44.841
A, was. B, did. C, has.

73
00:04:48.400 --> 00:04:50.028
The answer is A: was.

74
00:04:51.183 --> 00:04:54.669
A decision of a court at the end of a trial is a blank.

75
00:04:54.969 --> 00:04:58.680
A, suspect. B, verdict. C, investigation.

76
00:05:02.233 --> 00:05:04.395
The answer is B: verdict.

77
00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.327
Facts or objects that help prove something are blank.

78
00:05:08.627 --> 00:05:12.107
A, evidence. B, funding. C, a headline.

79
00:05:15.667 --> 00:05:17.550
The answer is A: evidence.

80
00:05:18.717 --> 00:05:22.957
Before a verdict, a careful reporter writes that the suspect is blank of theft.

81
00:05:23.257 --> 00:05:26.620
A, guilty. B, accused. C, convicted.

82
00:05:30.167 --> 00:05:32.178
The answer is B: accused.

83
00:05:33.317 --> 00:05:36.315
According to the reading, careful wording protects…

84
00:05:36.615 --> 00:05:42.288
A, only the police. B, public safety and fairness. C, newspaper sales.

85
00:05:45.833 --> 00:05:49.238
The answer is B: public safety and fairness.

86
00:05:50.433 --> 00:05:53.446
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:05:54.283 --> 00:05:58.791
Why must crime reporters be careful about how they describe a suspect before a trial?

88
00:06:07.583 --> 00:06:09.130
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:09.430 --> 00:06:34.019
Crime reporters must be careful because a suspect is still innocent until a court decides otherwise. If a newspaper calls someone guilty too early, it may destroy that person's reputation, even if the evidence later proves them innocent. Therefore, reporters use words such as 'accused' and 'allegedly'. This protects fairness and public trust in the news.

90
00:06:34.319 --> 00:06:36.075
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:06:36.983 --> 00:06:41.969
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and passive and careful reporting words.

92
00:06:42.269 --> 00:06:46.373
Today's value is justice. Stand up for what is fair for everyone.

93
00:06:46.673 --> 00:06:48.826
See you in Unit eight. Goodbye!

94
00:06:49.126 --> 00:06:52.057
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

